# [DD-13b.W23.3] D99's PARENTHESIS AS A CHECK: an aux block is parsed
# STRUCTURALLY, so a malformed one is a LOCAL error and cannot corrupt
# what follows.
#
# The `weight` line below is mis-indented: at indent 3 it is SHALLOWER
# than `seed` (5) and DEEPER than `roster` (2), so it closes back to a
# level that was never open and attaches to nothing. All three legs
# refuse with class `structure-attachment`, naming the MIS-INDENTED LINE.
#
# THE SHAPE HAD TO BE A RAGGED DEDENT AND NOT A DEEP INDENT, which is a
# fact about the OPEN SUBTREE worth stating where the fixture is: inside
# a subtree ANY deeper indent is a legal child, because the subtree has
# no rows and therefore no kind that "takes no continuation". The first
# version of this file indented a line further than its neighbour and was
# ACCEPTED — correctly. What a free tree can still get wrong is closing
# back to a level nobody opened, and that is the one structural error an
# aux body has left.
#
# HEADLESS ON PURPOSE, so all three legs can answer: `ext` at BLOCK scope
# is a body line, where legs B and C have their own parsers. The FILE
# scope is the head and the head has one parser, which is why
# `aux_arbitrary_keys.rxtin` is leg A only.
#
# THE ASSERTION THAT MATTERS IS THE SECOND HALF: the `pattern b+` block
# below is NOT implicated. A refusal that names the right line proves the
# error is local, which is the entire argument for parsing an aux body's
# STRUCTURE rather than treating it as opaque bytes — opaque bytes have
# no line to blame and no end anyone can find.
pattern a+
m "aaa" 0 3
ext bench
  roster pcre2 re2
     seed 20260915
   weight 3
  policy strict

pattern b+
m "bbb" 0 3
